---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import LockDemo from "../components/LockDemo.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, LOCK_BASE, SITE_TITLE, X_HANDLE, X_URL } from "../consts";

const usage = `# Capture the response first: piping curl into jq would hide curl's exit
# status, and a 409 would silently leave TOKEN empty and run the job anyway.
RESPONSE=$(curl -sf -X POST "${LOCK_BASE}/v1/locks/nightly-import?ttl=60&owner=$HOSTNAME" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY") || { echo "someone else has it"; exit 0; }

TOKEN=$(jq -r .token <<< "$RESPONSE")
trap 'curl -sf -X DELETE "${LOCK_BASE}/v1/locks/nightly-import?token=$TOKEN" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY" > /dev/null' EXIT

./import.sh`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`NanoLock — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="A lock over HTTP: a lease with a TTL, a token only the holder knows, and a fencing counter. Stops two copies of a job running at once."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">nano-api / lock</p>
					<h1>Two instances woke up.<br /><span class="dim">Only one should run the job.</span></h1>
					<p class="lede">
						<code>flock</code> over HTTP. One call says whether you got it, and nobody else gets it
						until you let go or your lease runs out.
					</p>
					<p class="cta">
						<a class="button" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=nano-api%20key`}>Get a free API key</a>
						<a class="button secondary" href="/howto/#lock">Curl walkthrough</a>
					</p>
					<p class="fineprint muted">
						Free. Mail <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or ping
						<a href={X_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{X_HANDLE}</a> on X.
					</p>
				</div>
				<LockDemo />
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>The whole thing, in a shell script</h2>
				<pre><code>{usage}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted small">
					<code>curl -sf</code> exits non-zero on 409, so the second instance stops. The
					<code>trap</code> releases the lock however the script ends, crash included. Capture the
					response before <code>jq</code> sees it: in a pipeline, <code>||</code> would test jq's
					exit status, not curl's.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Where this saves you</h2>
				<div class="two">
					<div class="card">
						<p class="tag">Without a lock</p>
						<ul class="plain">
							<li>Two pods wake at 03:00, both run the import</li>
							<li>A slow run overlaps the next one</li>
							<li>A retry fires while the original is still working</li>
							<li>You add a flag column and get the race wrong</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="card alive-edge">
						<p class="tag live">With NanoLock</p>
						<ul class="plain">
							<li>One atomic call decides who proceeds</li>
							<li>The lease expires by itself if the holder dies</li>
							<li>Only the holder can release it</li>
							<li>You can see who holds what, and until when</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>What the guarantee is — and is not</h2>
				<p>
					Acquiring is a single atomic write that only overwrites an expired lease. Two callers in
					the same millisecond cannot both win; twenty parallel requests produce exactly one, which
					is a test in the repo rather than a claim on a page.
				</p>
				<p>
					A lease is not consensus. Stall past your TTL — long GC, suspended VM — and someone else
					legitimately acquires while you still think you hold it.
				</p>
				<p>
					That is what <strong>fence</strong> is for: a number that only ever increases. Give it to
					whatever you are protecting, have that reject anything older, and a late holder is turned
					away on arrival.
				</p>
				<p class="muted small">
					In order of importance: renew while you work, keep the TTL above your worst-case runtime,
					use the fence where being wrong would cost something.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Pairs with the others</h2>
				<p>
					Lock the job <a href="/relay/">Relay</a> calls, ping <a href="/pulse/">Pulse</a> when it
					finishes. Runs, runs once, and you hear about it if it stops.
				</p>
				<p class="cta">
					<a class="button" href="/howto/">How to, in curl</a>
					<a class="button secondary" href="/pricing/">What it costs</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.hero {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1.05fr 1fr;
		gap: 2.5em;
		align-items: center;
		padding: 2em 0 1em;
	}
	.dim {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--muted);
		max-width: 34em;
	}
	.cta {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.7em;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin: 1.4em 0 0.6em;
	}
	.fineprint {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.band {
		padding: 3em 0 0;
	}
	.band > p {
		max-width: 46em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.small {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.two {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 1.2em;
		margin-top: 1.4em;
	}
	.alive-edge {
		border-color: var(--alive-dim);
	}
	ul.plain {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 1em 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
	}
	ul.plain li {
		padding-left: 1.3em;
		position: relative;
		margin-bottom: 0.6em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	ul.plain li::before {
		content: "·";
		position: absolute;
		left: 0.3em;
	}
	.alive-edge ul.plain li::before {
		content: "✓";
		color: var(--alive);
		left: 0;
	}
	@media (max-width: 860px) {
		.hero,
		.two {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
